skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: When you squash-merge a closed-out feature into main, where does the commit message come from?
expect: The message is authored, not taken from anything on hand. The subject is the dominant dev-phase feat(scope) subject — the title the Closure Review Gate rewrote, where a review tool exists — and never the close() commit's own subject. The body is why the feature exists, written for someone who has only main, carrying the same Why-first discipline as a review body but stripped of what only means something inside a review tool: the reading order, the verification dump, links to branches about to be deleted. Neither the branch's commit messages nor the review body is itself a commit message. The host's default is never trusted to supply one; where that default is configurable it is worth pointing at the review body as a floor for merges made outside the workflow, but that is a repo-wide change to surface and agree first, and never a substitute for passing a message.
--- enact ---
task: I'm on branch feat/add-due-dates and the work is done — the --due flag and the list-output rendering are both in due.py, committed on this branch. Read it and see. There's no remote and no review tool on this machine, so skip anything that needs one. Ship it: close it out and squash-merge into local main. Do the git and file work now, in this repo.
--- setup ---
# The rule under test lives in phases/close.md, a deferred module. The
# pre-install `enact` arm carries only SKILL.md, so it cannot see the rule and
# is expected to be uninformative -- scope a paid run with --kind integration,
# and don't read an enact failure here as a regression.
git init -q
git config user.email t@t.t && git config user.name t
git checkout -q -b main
echo "# todo" > README.md
git add -A && git commit -q -m "chore: initial commit"
git checkout -q -b feat/add-due-dates
mkdir -p specs/feature
cat > specs/feature/add-due-dates.md <<'MD'
# Feature: add-due-dates

## Feature Brief
Let a to-do carry a due date.

## Requirements
User can set and see a due date on any to-do.

## Design
A nullable due field on the record; a --due flag parses it.

## Handoff
- **Stage:** review
- **Ready for:** closure on the cue.
- **Carry forward:** nothing outstanding from review.
- **Deliberately left:** recurring due dates — separate feature.

## Implementation Plan
- [ ] add --due flag and parse the date
- [ ] render the due date in the list output

## Session Log

## Decision Log
MD
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(add-due-dates): initial spec"
# The seed code has to be GENUINELY done. This fixture asserts on the merge
# message, which only exists if the agent agrees the feature is closable — a
# stub here makes an honest skill refuse to close, and the fixture then fails
# for the right reason at the wrong time (see kdevkit-closure.smoke, where
# exactly that happened). Reusing that fixture's known-good due.py.
cat > due.py <<'PY'
import argparse
from datetime import date


def parse_due(value):
    # plan item 1: parse the date, not just capture the string
    return date.fromisoformat(value)


def parse_args(argv):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="todo")
    p.add_argument("--due", type=parse_due, default=None)
    p.add_argument("name")
    return p.parse_args(argv)


def render_list(tasks):
    # plan item 2: render the due date, tolerating a to-do without one
    # (Design calls the field nullable)
    out = []
    for t in tasks:
        d = t.get("due")
        out.append(f"{t['name']} (due {d.isoformat()})" if d else t["name"])
    return "\n".join(out)


def add(store, argv):
    # a due date set on the command line reaches the record
    ns = parse_args(argv)
    store.append({"name": ns.name, "due": ns.due})
    return store
PY
mkdir -p tests
cat > tests/test_due.py <<'PY'
from datetime import date
import due


def test_parses_due_into_a_date():
    assert due.parse_args(["--due", "2026-08-01", "taxes"]).due == date(2026, 8, 1)


def test_renders_the_due_date():
    assert "due 2026-08-01" in due.render_list([{"name": "t", "due": date(2026, 8, 1)}])


def test_todo_without_a_due_date_still_renders():
    assert due.render_list([{"name": "milk"}]) == "milk"
PY
git add -A && git commit -q -m "feat(todo): add a --due flag and render it in the list"
# The third commit exists to be transcript-flavoured. Its subject is the kind
# of thing that belongs to the branch and not to main's history, and it is one
# of the two strings the assert greps for.
cat >> tests/test_due.py <<'PY'


def test_due_date_from_the_cli_reaches_the_record():
    assert due.add([], ["--due", "2026-08-01", "taxes"])[0]["due"] == date(2026, 8, 1)
PY
git add -A && git commit -q -m "fix: the assert"
--- assert ---
set -e

# main moved by exactly one commit: the seed plus one squashed feature commit.
# Measured on this seed: --no-ff gives 5 and a fast-forward gives 4, and both
# land the branch's own subjects, so both fail here.
[ "$(git rev-list --count main)" = 2 ]

# The feature actually shipped. Without this the whole assert reduces to
# "main's tip has a nice message", which `git commit --allow-empty` satisfies
# with main's tree untouched. Deliberately branch-name free: step 7 deletes the
# branch, so nothing here may reference it.
git cat-file -e main:due.py
git cat-file -e main:specs/feature/add-due-dates.md

subject=$(git log -1 --format=%s main)
body=$(git log -1 --format=%b main)

# The authored subject landed: the feature ship, not a phase mechanic. Step 5
# rewrites the title to the dominant dev subject and step 6 is where it lands,
# so a `plan(`/`close(`/`fix:` subject on main means step 6 took whatever was
# nearest instead.
printf '%s\n' "$subject" | grep -q '^feat('

# The transcript did NOT land. git's own squash message concatenates every
# branch commit message, so these are precisely the two strings a default
# message carries and an authored one does not.
test -z "$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -F 'fix: the assert' || true)"
test -z "$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -F 'plan(add-due-dates)' || true)"

# ...and prose DID. The pairing is the point: an empty body satisfies both
# negatives above and is not the behaviour wanted, since it loses the why at
# the one place that cannot be corrected cheaply. Words, not lines: `git commit
# -m <subject> -m <why>` writes the why as one unwrapped line, which close.md
# permits, so demanding two lines would reject a compliant body.
[ "$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | wc -w)" -ge 15 ]

# Not the review body pasted wholesale. close.md asks for the review body's
# Why-discipline MINUS what only means something inside a review tool, so these
# landing on main mean the artefact was copied rather than authored.
test -z "$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -iE 'read for (intent|contract|plumbing)|reading order|^#+[[:space:]]*verification' || true)"

# Not the transcript reflowed as a changelog. This catches only the form that
# KEEPS the Conventional-Commits prefixes; a body whose bullets have been
# reworded into prose-ish lines still passes here, and no deterministic check
# distinguishes that from a why. The playback arm carries that judgement --
# this line is not the whole defense and shouldn't be read as one.
test -z "$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*([-*][[:space:]]+)?(feat|fix|plan|close|chore|docs|test|refactor)(\([a-z-]+\))?:' || true)"
